  "headline": "Prime Minister Tarique Rahman conducts meetings during first UN General Assembly visit in New York",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Bangladesh Prime Minister Tarique Rahman leveraged his first visit to the United Nations General Assembly in New York to showcase the country's potential and present his national plans, according to Irene Khan, Bangladesh’s permanent representative to the UN. Speaking to The Daily Star, Khan stated that Rahman attended the high-level event from a Monday evening until a Friday evening, conducting approximately 30 meetings within that four-day window. These engagements included 10 meetings with UN agencies, one regarding the Rohingya issue, two focused on climate change, and a major meeting with investors. Khan described the visit as a successful debut for the relatively newly elected Prime Minister, noting that delegations were eager to understand his vision and the direction of Bangladesh."
    },
    {
      "text": "During the visit, Rahman communicated a message that Bangladesh seeks international cooperation and is willing to work with all parties without being beholden to any. Khan noted that Rahman emphasized the country is open for business and seeking investment, specifically highlighting plans for investment in social development and the upskilling of women and youth. Khan characterized the reception of the Prime Minister as a warm welcome, occurring at a time when she observed powerful states challenging international consensus and the UN."
    }
  ]
}

